Video players must adapt bitrate quickly to avoid stalls while using available capacity, yet ABR decisions rely on delayed user-space signals. We present eBandit, an eBPF prototype that moves online ABR-policy selection into the kernel using a small multi-armed bandit, while leaving bitrate computation in the player. In live eBPF trace-replay evaluation, eBandit improves adversarial-trace QoE by 12.0% over the best static baseline, attains the highest mean QoE on 42 Norway HSDPA sessions while matching or beating the best static ABR in every session and improving 28.6% of them, with control-path overhead of only tens of microseconds.